<?nvs
// ADR 0086 § 6's compile-time command table, as a program.
//
// Structurally identical to ADR 0077's route table with the routes swapped for
// commands. `Core\Program::implementing<T>()` builds it, so the `autoload`
// root is what finds the classes nothing names.
autoload 'App' from './commands';

// § 6's own readers of the table are `Core\Command::run`, `::help` and
// `::completions`. `run` dispatches and is out of this goal's scope, and the
// other two answer a `Core\Cli\Text`, which carries no member until M8
// (`crates/nvs-stdlib/src/out.rs`) — so this program reads the table's
// *classes* through the enumeration the table is itself built from, and asks
// each for the usage line its `#[Command]` declares.
//
// That is what makes this fixture assert something rather than echo three
// frozen strings: the enumeration is what parses and checks the two
// declarations, so § 6's three compile errors — a duplicate command name, two
// options sharing a spelling, an `#[Option]` on a parameter whose declared
// type has no conversion from `string` — are reachable from here.
var $commands = Core\Program::implementing<App\Command>();

// The list is sorted by fully-qualified name. `App\Greet` comes before
// `App\Migrate`, and the order of files on disk does not change it.
foreach ($commands as App\Command $command) {
    echo $command->usage(), "\n";
}

echo 'commands=', Core\Arr::count($commands), "\n";
